            "ID": "wolfi",
            "Name": "Wolfi Secdb",
            "URL": "https://packages.wolfi.dev/os/security.json"
          },
          "Title": "nodejs: nodejs: dns.resolve truncates hostnames containing a NUL character",
          "Description": "A flaw in Node.js DNS resolution allows hostnames containing a NUL byte to be silently truncated by the dns.resolve*() functions and dns.reverse(). This is an incomplete fix of CVE-2026-48930, which only covered dns.lookup() and the net/tls host options.\r\n\r\nBecause the name is passed to c-ares as a C string, resolving attacker.example\\0.trusted.example queries attacker.example and returns its records without any error. Applications that validate a hostname, for example against a domain allowlist, and then resolve it with dns.resolve*() can act on records for a different name than the one they validated.\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability affects Node.js 26.x, 24.x, and 22.x.",
          "Severity": "HIGH",
          "CweIDs": [
            "CWE-284"
          ],
          "VendorSeverity": {
            "amazon": 3,
            "redhat": 3
          },
          "CVSS": {
